WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.990
With first Meg and then Amy gone, the house seemed empty and desolate.

2
00:00:13.991 --> 00:00:19.430
Beth must have felt it too, Jo thought, because her younger sister seemed more subdued lately.

3
00:00:19.431 --> 00:00:26.961
Watching her now as they hung out the laundry on the clothesline in the yard, Jo sensed something was definitely amiss.

4
00:00:26.962 --> 00:00:33.110
"Beth, how are you sleeping lately?" Jo asked, clipping two clothespins on Father's undershirt.

5
00:00:33.111 --> 00:00:38.523
"Fine, Jo. I'm fine," Beth answered breathlessly.

6
00:00:38.524 --> 00:00:43.682
Then looking out across the yard onto the Laurences' garden, Beth let out a short sigh.

7
00:00:43.683 --> 00:00:48.692
Laurie was kicking a ball around, probably imagining it was someone's head.

8
00:00:48.693 --> 00:00:56.476
Jo smiled as she remembered how furious Mr. Laurence had been when he heard that his grandson had been suspended from college.

9
00:00:56.477 --> 00:01:02.556
It wasn't a laughing matter, but Laurie had told her about his grandfather's reactions so vividly,

10
00:01:02.557 --> 00:01:06.789
imitating his voice and gestures, that Jo couldn't help giggling.

11
00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:14.181
"Look at what a handsome man our boy has turned into," Beth said in a soft voice as she kept her eye on Laurie.

12
00:01:14.182 --> 00:01:21.298
Jo, suddenly feeling uneasy, thought she might have figured out why Beth had been so quiet these past few weeks.

13
00:01:21.299 --> 00:01:26.455
"She's in love with Laurie!" Jo screamed silently.

14
00:01:26.456 --> 00:01:31.060
Around the same time but in an entirely different part of the world,

15
00:01:31.061 --> 00:01:37.160
Amy was walking arm-in-arm with Aunt March along the famous Piccadilly Circus in London, England.

16
00:01:37.161 --> 00:01:47.295
Amy was dressed smartly in a well-fitted stylish dress with matching hat and parasol and taking small dainty steps so as to keep pace with her elderly aunt.

17
00:01:47.296 --> 00:01:55.234
Looking very prim and ladylike, Amy was noticed by the gentlemen passing by, some of them even stopping to offer compliments.

18
00:01:55.235 --> 00:02:00.990
"Did you know, Aunt March, that the name Piccadilly comes from a frilly collar called a 'picadil,'

19
00:02:00.991 --> 00:02:04.076
which was quite popular during the seventeenth century?

20
00:02:04.077 --> 00:02:09.219
That's why there are so many dressmakers along this street," Amy said knowledgeably.

21
00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:14.050
Glowing with pride, Aunt March said, "How clever you are, my dear."

22
00:02:14.051 --> 00:02:18.483
Then leading Amy to a very distinguished dressmaker's shop, she said,

23
00:02:18.484 --> 00:02:26.338
"If Piccadilly is famous for its fashions, then that is what we must get for you while we are here."

24
00:02:26.339 --> 00:02:32.066
Hardly able to contain her excitement, Amy followed her aunt into the shop.

25
00:02:32.067 --> 00:02:38.841
Back home Jo was going into a shop of a different sort, the fruit and vegetable kind.

26
00:02:38.842 --> 00:02:47.924
Since Beth did not have much of an appetite these days (as Jo found out from Meg, many lovesick people suffer the same symptoms),

27
00:02:47.925 --> 00:02:52.827
Jo wanted to buy some of Beth's favorite fruits and surprise her that evening.

28
00:02:52.828 --> 00:02:58.623
As she was choosing a couple of ripe apples, a familiar voice called to her from behind.

29
00:02:58.624 --> 00:03:05.386
"Jo! Fancy meeting you here!" Laurie shouted so loudly that the entire shop could hear.

30
00:03:05.387 --> 00:03:15.298
"Shush, Laurie! Everyone's staring," Jo whispered, her face turning as red as the apple in her hand. "What are you doing here, anyway?"

31
00:03:15.299 --> 00:03:21.966
"I felt like getting some fruit to munch on," Laurie said, tossing an apple into the air and catching it.

32
00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:31.719
"Don't be silly. You have servants to do your shopping," Jo said, pointing at the apple, "and besides, I happen to know that you don't like apples."

33
00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.021
Laurie held up both hands as if to surrender and said,

34
00:03:35.022 --> 00:03:42.249
"I've been cooped up in the house, bored out of my mind, and then I saw you leaving the house and thought to myself:

35
00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:46.446
That's odd—the old girl hasn't been around lately."

36
00:03:46.447 --> 00:03:50.066
Jo placed a hand over his mouth to stop his rambling.

37
00:03:50.067 --> 00:03:56.543
"As you can see I'm very busy right now, but perhaps I'll come over later, if I have time," she said.

38
00:03:56.544 --> 00:04:02.165
Shaking his head, Laurie said, "Mmm . . . bloof . . . mmhffoo," through Jo's hand.

39
00:04:02.166 --> 00:04:05.430
Removing her hand, Jo said, "What?"

40
00:04:05.431 --> 00:04:12.425
"I said no. You've been avoiding me and I want to know why," Laurie repeated more clearly this time.

41
00:04:12.426 --> 00:04:17.945
"I haven't been avoiding you," Jo said as she turned her back on him and walked away.

42
00:04:17.946 --> 00:04:27.962
"See? You're doing it again, Jo. I demand to know what I've done to deserve such treatment from you," he said, pretending to be insulted.

43
00:04:27.963 --> 00:04:36.659
"It's not you; it's Beth," Jo wanted to say but instead she said, "Would you mind holding this basket like a real gentleman?

44
00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:39.701
By the way, when are you going back to school?"

45
00:04:39.702 --> 00:04:45.945
With one hand, Laurie held the basket but with the other he grasped Jo's hand tightly.

46
00:04:45.946 --> 00:04:53.395
"Jo, why do you keep running? I'm getting tired of chasing you," he said in a very serious voice.

47
00:04:53.396 --> 00:04:56.919
As much as Jo had been trying to ignore the signs,

48
00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:03.798
there was no denying now that Laurie was indeed in love with one of the March sisters, but it certainly was not Beth.

49
00:05:03.799 --> 00:05:07.200
That evening, Jo sat in the living room with Mother.

50
00:05:07.201 --> 00:05:14.693
Beth had already gone up to bed, saying she had a headache, and Father was visiting a farmer who had lost his son in the war.

51
00:05:14.694 --> 00:05:21.761
"Is something troubling you, dear?" Mother always seemed to know when her girls were weighed down by problems.

52
00:05:21.762 --> 00:05:29.836
Jo deliberated whether to discuss Beth and Laurie with Mother, then decided it would be for the best and told her everything.

53
00:05:29.837 --> 00:05:36.180
Mother listened without speaking. Then she said, "That's the trouble with love; it's uncontrollable.

54
00:05:36.181 --> 00:05:39.863
It never goes in the direction that you want it to."

55
00:05:39.864 --> 00:05:44.609
"Everything would be solved if Laurie would love Beth," exclaimed Jo.

56
00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:49.239
"Are you sure of Beth's feelings for Laurie? Have you spoken to her?

57
00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:54.021
Perhaps it's something else and you are jumping to conclusions," Mother said.

58
00:05:54.022 --> 00:05:59.572
"I haven't asked her about it, but what else could it be?" Jo asked in a frustrated voice.

59
00:05:59.573 --> 00:06:03.763
"What are your feelings for Laurie?" Mother asked gently.

60
00:06:03.764 --> 00:06:07.705
"I love Laurie with all my heart but not like that.

61
00:06:07.706 --> 00:06:11.596
He's my best friend, my brother, but . . ." Jo paused.

62
00:06:11.597 --> 00:06:14.454
"I don't love him like Meg loves John."

63
00:06:14.455 --> 00:06:18.189
"Has Laurie told you how he feels?" asked Mother.

64
00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:24.916
Shaking her head, Jo answered, "No, but I'm afraid he's going to very soon and I will break his heart.

65
00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:32.018
And if Beth finds out, then her heart will be broken. Oh, what a mess."

66
00:06:32.019 --> 00:06:38.572
Mother held Jo in her arms for a while and then said, "What do you think is the solution to this problem?"

67
00:06:38.573 --> 00:06:47.007
"I was thinking," Jo began. "Your friend Mrs. Kirke wrote that she is looking for a governess for her two girls in New York, didn't she?"

68
00:06:47.008 --> 00:06:49.979
Mother nodded and told her to go on.

69
00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:58.200
"Well, what if I could apply for the position, just for the winter for now and see whether or not it's a suitable place for me.

70
00:06:58.201 --> 00:07:05.593
I have become restless and thought of traveling myself, so this might be the best time to do so," said Jo.

71
00:07:05.594 --> 00:07:09.292
"Yes, it does sound like a good idea," said Mother.

72
00:07:09.293 --> 00:07:17.136
"But Jo," she added, looking deep into her daughter's eyes, "running away from a problem doesn't solve it."

73
00:07:17.137 --> 00:07:23.576
"I'm not running away. I'm buying time so that I will know how to straighten out the situation best.

74
00:07:23.577 --> 00:07:30.148
And who knows, maybe while I'm gone, Laurie will fall madly in love with Beth," Jo said hopefully.

75
00:07:30.149 --> 00:07:35.344
"I'll write to Mrs. Kirke immediately," Mother said.
